Z||000004||HF6582||02-12-2000||05:43 PM||||what does your RPO code say.<BR>I'll have to look on mine, but i lnow it isn't WS6.<P>Scott||||reg||1
Z||000005||daved||02-12-2000||11:04 PM||||86GT, no WS6 on mine! Has the N90 code, which is for 15x7 Al. wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||02-13-2000||12:08 PM||||The meaning of some codes seems to change from year to year.....<P>I don't know what's in my 87SC. It doesn't list WS-6, but it's still allot stiffer than the base coupes. Stiff enough that the only place im considering pully bushings is the cradle and the sway. There is no sign that the previous owners did anything to it either other than taking pretty good care of it.<P>Even if you have WS-6, the option is meaningless if the shocks and/or struts are changed. Likely the new ones you get will be better anyway than what WS-6 used in the 80's. This alone can make a base car handle very close to what a WS-6 would.(Yes I know WS-6 also is suposed to use diferant rate springs.)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Formula||02-13-2000||12:27 PM||||I don't know about that websites info.<P>Apparently my formula should toast a 1984 corvette to 60mph. 6.9 to 7.5 or something.<P>I think I saw that all 88 formula and Gts had the ws6 suspension.  I alwyas thought thats why the 88s were better.<P>also.....it says the 86se gets higher skidpad ratings then the 88Gt and is the same as the 88 Formula,  I don't think he tested with perfect condition cars if the 88s don't have better skidpad ratings. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||02-13-2000||03:33 PM||||Tires have more bearing on skid pad than anything else. The ealier car may have had tires more suited to the test than the other.||||reg||1
